@page "/"

<PageTitle>BlazorScanner</PageTitle>

<h1>BlazorScanner</h1>
<p class="lede">
    A barcode scanner for Blazor with the decoding written from scratch in C#. No barcode SDK, no
    JavaScript scanner library, no component framework. The browser opens the camera and copies
    pixels; everything after that is managed code.
</p>

<div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-block-start:1.5rem;">
    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-block-start:0;">Nine symbologies</h3>
        <p style="margin:0;color:var(--fg-muted);font-size:0.9rem;">
            QR Code, Data Matrix, Code 128, Code 39, EAN-13, EAN-8, UPC-A, UPC-E and ITF, each
            decoded by a reader written against the specification. PDF417 is implemented but needs
            its specification symbol table.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-block-start:0;">One copy per frame</h3>
        <p style="margin:0;color:var(--fg-muted);font-size:0.9rem;">
            JavaScript writes the frame straight into managed memory through a memory view. There
            is no JSON, no base64 and no second buffer.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-block-start:0;">No garbage per frame</h3>
        <p style="margin:0;color:var(--fg-muted);font-size:0.9rem;">
            Buffers, bit planes and scan lines are all reused. A frame with no barcode in it
            allocates nothing at all.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Try it</h2>
<div class="split">
    <div>
        <BarcodeScanner Formats="BarcodeFormat.All"
                        Region="ScanRegion.CenteredSquare(0.75)"
                        OnScan="OnScan" />
        <p class="field__hint" style="margin-block-start:0.6rem;">
            Needs HTTPS or localhost, and camera permission. No camera to hand? Use
            <a href="image">image scanning</a> or open the
            <a href="samples">sample barcodes</a> on another screen.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
        @if (_last is not null)
        {
            <div class="card">
                <h3 style="margin-block-start:0;">Last scan</h3>
                <p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><span class="pill">@_last.Format</span></p>
                <pre style="margin:0;"><code>@_last.Text</code></pre>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="card">
                <p class="empty" style="padding:0.5rem;">Scan something to see the strongly typed result here.</p>
            </div>
        }

        <div class="card">
            <h3 style="margin-block-start:0;">Quick start</h3>
            <pre><code>&lt;BarcodeScanner Formats="BarcodeFormat.QrCode | BarcodeFormat.Ean13"
                Region="ScanRegion.CenteredSquare(0.7)"
                OnScan="HandleScan" /&gt;

@@code {
    private void HandleScan(BarcodeResult result)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{result.Format}: {result.Text}");
    }
}</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Installation</h2>
<pre><code>dotnet add package BlazorScanner</code></pre>
<p>Then reference the stylesheet from <code>index.html</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/BlazorScanner/blazor-scanner.css" /&gt;</code></pre>
<p>
    That is the whole setup. There is no service registration, no JavaScript tag and no
    initialisation call: the component loads its own module the first time it renders.
</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Where</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Capture</td>
            <td>Browser</td>
            <td><code>getUserMedia</code> feeds a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>; a canvas scales frames to the processing size.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Transfer</td>
            <td>Boundary</td>
            <td>One <code>JSImport</code> call copies RGBA into a reused managed buffer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Luminance</td>
            <td>C#</td>
            <td>Fixed point BT.601 weights, with optional subsampling in the same pass.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Binarise</td>
            <td>C#</td>
            <td>Adaptive 8×8 block thresholds for matrix codes, per-row histograms for linear ones.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Detect</td>
            <td>C#</td>
            <td>Finder pattern search, perspective transform and grid sampling.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Decode</td>
            <td>C#</td>
            <td>Reed-Solomon correction, de-interleaving and bit stream parsing.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

@code {
    private BarcodeResult? _last;

    private void OnScan(BarcodeResult result)
    {
        _last = result;
        StateHasChanged();
    }
}
